<Layout title="About — Jack Bush">
    <main>
        <section>
            <h1>Background</h1>
            <p>I grew up in New Zealand, trained as an industrial designer, then spent twenty years doing something adjacent to that — building digital products, running product teams, and occasionally getting too close to the code.</p>
            <p>I've been a web developer, a product manager, a head of product and a VP. I've worked at agencies and startups. I've helped build things that didn't exist before and helped rescue things that had lost their way.</p>
            <p>The thread through all of it is an interest in the earliest stage — before the product has a shape, when the decisions you make will set the direction for everything that follows. That's where I think most clearly, and where I do my best work.</p>
            <p>I'm currently based between France and New Zealand. I work with clients everywhere.</p>
        </section>

        <section>
            <h2>Point of view</h2>
            <h3>Most early-stage products fail for the same reason.</h3>
            <p>Not because the idea was wrong. Not because the market wasn't there. Because the team spent too long building the wrong version of the right thing.</p>
            <p>The failure mode I see most often: a founder with a genuine insight, surrounded by smart people, building a product that gets more complex with every sprint and less connected to the original problem. By the time they notice, they've shipped something that kind of works but doesn't quite fit — and refactoring the product is almost as hard as starting over.</p>
            <p>The fix isn't better process. It's someone in the room who can hold the original problem and the current build in their head at the same time, and say <em>we've drifted</em> before it's too late.</p>
            <p>That's what I'm for.</p>
            <p>Good early-stage product work is mostly editing. It's saying no to the right things. It's knowing which decision is reversible and which one isn't. It's understanding that a product that does one thing well will always beat a product that does six things adequately.</p>
            <p>I've learned this the hard way, more than once. Now I try to help other people avoid learning it that way.</p>
        </section>
    </main>
</Layout>
